  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nSensible weather remains minimal through the extended.\n\nCalm, to subtle south/southeast winds tonight and temps \ndropping to saturation will lead to areas of fog. Best chance \nwill be near Saginaw Bay extending into portions of northeast \nlower. Lesser chance west of 75, generally, but still patchy fog\ncannot be ruled out. Otherwise, a quiet night is expected. \nWednesday, anomalous upper heights across the Great Lakes will \nget beat down some due to a passing disturbance to the north \nacross Ontario. Depending on how far deep that feature digs, it \nmay try to scrape far northern portions of the area. \nNevertheless, POPs are <10% across the E UP. Sfc high pressure \nbuilds in behind this feature, becoming a touch cooler on \nThursday, due to the northeast wind shift. \n\nUpper heights quickly rise during the end of the week and into the \nfirst half of the weekend. As such, temperatures moderate back into \nthe 60s, mid 60s Saturday, with southerly winds. Outside of the \nunseasonable warm conditions, expect precipitation free weather as \nwell.\n\nEnergy aloft that invaded the western portions of North America this \nweek will begin to eject eastward with time, especially late this \nweekend. Consequently, heights get shunted eastward and squashed \nsouthward. West and southwest flow aloft will likely advect a \ncorridor of moisture northward across the Upper Midwest, and thus, \nthe chance for a few showers across northern Michigan. That is \nassuming the moisture/low level boundary push far enough south, \nwhich would result in a cooldown as well early next week. The actual \npush of coolest air with this cold front looks to be around the \nTuesday time frame, as the main upper trough swings in the vicinity \nof the area. Thus, although  a cooldown is in store on Monday, the \ncoolest temps will likely be on Tuesday. The actual precipitation\nwith this feature is still very much a question mark at this \ntime. Still, no significant weather is upon us during this \ntime based on the latest guidance.",
